theory.intro:
The **tangent-chord angle** lies between a tangent and a chord drawn from the point of contact. It equals the angle in the **alternate segment** — the angle at any point on the arc on the other side of that chord.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw (O) circle (2.2);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below] at (O) {$O$};
\coordinate (T) at (0,2.2);
\coordinate (L) at (-1.4,2.2);
\coordinate (R) at (1.4,2.2);
\coordinate (A) at (-60:2.2);
\coordinate (B) at (210:2.2);
\draw (L) -- (R);
\node[above] at (T) {$T$};
\node[right] at (A) {$A$};
\node[left] at (B) {$B$};
\draw (T) -- (A);
\draw (T) -- (B);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\pic[draw, "$\alpha$", angle radius=0.6cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = A--T--R};
\pic[draw, "$\alpha$", angle radius=0.5cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = A--B--T};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
The **alternate segment** is the segment on the opposite side of the chord from the tangent-chord angle, not the segment beside it.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (T) at (0,2.2);
\coordinate (L) at (-1.4,2.2);
\coordinate (R) at (1.4,2.2);
\coordinate (A) at (-60:2.2);
\fill[gray!25] (A) arc[start angle=-60, end angle=-270, radius=2.2] -- cycle;
\draw (O) circle (2.2);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below] at (O) {$O$};
\draw (L) -- (R);
\node[above] at (T) {$T$};
\node[right] at (A) {$A$};
\draw (T) -- (A);
\pic[draw, "$\alpha$", angle radius=0.6cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = A--T--R};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The chord makes two supplementary tangent-chord angles at $T$, one on each side, and each matches the angle in the segment across the chord.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (T) at (0,2.2);
\coordinate (L) at (-1.4,2.2);
\coordinate (R) at (1.4,2.2);
\coordinate (A) at (-60:2.2);
\fill[gray!25] (T) arc[start angle=90, end angle=-60, radius=2.2] -- cycle;
\draw (O) circle (2.2);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below] at (O) {$O$};
\draw (L) -- (R);
\node[above] at (T) {$T$};
\node[right] at (A) {$A$};
\draw (T) -- (A);
\pic[draw, "$\beta$", angle radius=0.6cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = L--T--A};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

